Business Understanding
This phase establishes the project's overall objectives and success criteria.
Business Objectives 
The overarching business problem is employee turnover (attrition). The objective is to help the company move from reacting to turnover to preventing it.
Goal: Strengthen workforce retention strategies by identifying meaningful patterns in employee attrition.
Outcome: Design targeted interventions and implement organizational changes grounded in data-driven evidence.
Value: Quantify the cost of turnover and highlight high-risk roles, making retention efforts easier to justify.
Data Mining Goal 
The Data Mining Goal is to uncover the key drivers of attrition and predict employees at risk of leaving.
Prediction: Identify who is most at risk of leaving and the reasons behind it.
Discovery: Find out the real stories behind why employees leave, such as when low job satisfaction, long commutes, and constant overtime come together to push employees toward resignation.
Business Success Criteria 
Success will be measured by the ability of the models to provide actionable insights that lead to measurable improvements in retention metrics.
A successful model will enable HR to focus on real-life challenges and take action before employees decide to go.
The findings must be clear and translatable into practical decision points for managers.
Data Understanding
This phase focuses on collecting, describing, exploring, and verifying the quality of the data.
Collect Initial Data (Data Sources) 
The primary dataset for this project is the IBM HR Analytics Employee Attrition & Performance dataset.
Acquisition Method (for this project): The synthetic dataset is acquired externally from Kaggle, a reliable, publicly accessible source for academic and professional analytics.
Acquisition Method (in a real corporate setting): This type of data would be acquired internally from systems such as HRIS, payroll, performance management systems, and employee engagement systems.
Data Content: The dataset includes variables related to:
Employee Demographics (Age, gender, marital status, education) 
Job & Organizational Variables (Job role, job satisfaction, work-life balance) 
Compensation & Benefits (Monthly income, stock option level) 
Workplace Behavior (Distance from home, overtime status, total working years) 
Target Variable: Attrition Labels (Whether the employee left the company – Yes/No) 
Verify Data Quality 
Ensuring data quality, reliability, and suitability is critical before modeling.
Potential Issues Highlighted:
Missing values (gaps in fields like education or income).
Outliers (e.g., in monthly income or daily rate).
Inconsistent categories or incorrect data types.
Duplicate records or unbalanced classes (where most people stay while fewer leave).
Incorrect values (e.g., negative ages, "Years at Company" > "Total Working Years").
Quality Assessment Steps:
Identify and quantify missing data; impute numerical values (mean/median) or categorical values (mode/unknown category).
Check for and correct incorrect values, duplicated IDs, or inconsistent combinations.
Detect outliers for numerical fields, evaluate them, and either correct or remove them.
Ensure correct data types and document all cleaning decisions.
